'''CIEA''' ({{lang|ca|Corporació d'intercanvi d'energia d'Abyala}}) is a multinational {{w|integrated oil company|integrated petroleum company}} headquartered in [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]], and one of the world's largest [[supermajor (oil company)|supermajor]] oil corporations. The company operates across the full {{w|petroleum industry|oil and gas value chain}}, from {{w|upstream (oil industry)|upstream}} exploration and production to {{w|downstream (oil industry)|downstream}} refining, distribution, and petrochemicals. CIEA serves most of [[Abyala]], parts of [[Sinchew]], and [[Balisca]], and is one of the largest oil companies in Abyala by revenue.

The company is dual-listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] and the Sanropura Stock Exchange, with a {{w|market capitalization}} of approximately $140 billion.

== History ==

=== Founding and the Great Adonian War (1936–1948) ===

CIEA's origins date to 1936, when [[Francesc Reig i Ferrer]], a Balboan businessman with connections to the [[Volisania|Volisanian]] military, established '''Companyia Petrolífera Unida de Balboa''' (CPU Balboa) during the [[Great Adonian War]]. The company was created as a covert petroleum import group to secure oil supplies for the Balboan rump state and the Volisanian military in the Abyalan theater, sourcing crude from [[Ayorin]] and [[Sanyan]] through secret shipping routes that bypassed enemy naval blockades.

CPU Balboa's operations were dangerous and logistically complex, involving chartered tankers sailing under neutral flags and transshipment through intermediaries in the [[Bight of Ayorin]]. The company survived the war despite the destruction of much of Balboa's infrastructure, and emerged as one of the few functioning petroleum logistics firms in the post-war Abyalan theater.

=== Nationalisation as Petrolí de Balboa (1948–1974) ===

After the [[Great Adonian War]], the [[Adonian Community]] receivership administration nationalised CPU Balboa as part of the broader consolidation of strategic assets in the reconstructed Balboan state. The company was renamed '''Petrolí de Balboa''' (PB) and given a {{w|monopoly}} on petroleum refining and distribution in Balboa.

Under state ownership, PB built Balboa's first modern refinery at Badia Curta, on the Shendan coast in Jala province, in the early 1950s. As Badia Curta lay outside the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|Adonian Community mandate]] area, the Balboan rump state developed the site as an industrial centre to compete with the mandate-controlled Sant Cristòfor and canal corridor. PB expanded the country's fuel distribution network and supplied the growing Balboan economy with refined petroleum products. However, the company remained purely a downstream operator — a refiner and importer with no {{w|upstream (oil industry)|upstream}} production capabilities of its own.

=== Privatisation (1974) ===

During the [[1968–1978 Balboa recession|Balboan recession]], the Balboan government was forced to sell off state assets to raise revenue. Petrolí de Balboa was privatised in 1974, sold to a consortium of Balboan and international investors. The privatisation was controversial, mirroring the earlier debates over the Bolsa and telecom sales, but was driven by the government's urgent fiscal needs.

=== Merger and transformation (1996) ===

In 1996, Petrolí de Balboa merged with '''Syarikat Minyak Sanrobonian Selatan''' (SMSS), a Sanrobonian oil company with significant {{w|upstream (oil industry)|upstream}} production assets in southern Sanrobonia. The merger was transformative: it gave the combined company crude oil production capabilities for the first time, ending its six-decade dependence on imported oil. The merged entity was renamed '''CIEA''' (Corporació d'intercanvi d'energia d'Abyala), reflecting its new status as a pan-Abyalan integrated energy corporation rather than a purely Balboan refiner.

The dual listing on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] and the Sanropura Stock Exchange was established as part of the merger, reflecting the company's binational origins.

=== Expansion into Potocsí (2000s) ===

Following the merger, CIEA acquired exploration and production rights in the Kuluba basin in [[Potocsí]], one of the major oil-producing regions of the Marañón basin. The acquisition gave CIEA a foothold in one of Abyala's largest oil provinces and further diversified its upstream portfolio across Balboa, Sanrobonia, and Potocsí.

=== Balisca and the COBAL contract (2000s–present) ===

In the early 2000s, CIEA secured a long-term supply contract with COBAL, the Baliscan state oil import entity, to export refined petroleum products to [[Balisca]]. COBAL handles 100% of Balisca's oil imports, and CIEA supplies approximately 30% of Balisca's total oil consumption, making CIEA the largest foreign supplier to the Baliscan energy market.

The contract is a direct consequence of the Balboan economic model: CIEA imports crude oil cheaply (the overvalued ID makes crude imports inexpensive), refines it at its Badia Curta complex on the Shendan coast using low-cost hydropower from the Yalabirga dams, and exports the refined products to Balisca at competitive prices. Petroleum products are not covered by the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]], which applies only to agricultural products; CIEA's competitiveness derives entirely from the input-side advantage of cheap crude oil. The [[Balboa Canal]] and Sant Cristòfor harbour provide efficient logistics for the trade.

The COBAL contract has made CIEA a critical player in the Baliscan energy market and has given Balboa a degree of geopolitical leverage over Balisca, which depends on CIEA for nearly a third of its oil supply. The contract has been a major source of revenue growth for CIEA, contributing to the company's rise from the smallest supermajor to a mid-tier player among the world's largest oil companies.

== Operations ==

CIEA operates across the full petroleum value chain:

* '''Upstream''': Crude oil exploration and production in Sanrobonia (southern fields inherited from SMSS) and Potocsí (Kuluba basin). The company also holds natural gas assets.
* '''Downstream''': Petroleum refining at facilities in Badia Curta (Jala province, Shendan coast) and Sanrobonia, with a combined refining capacity of approximately 1.5 million barrels per day. The Badia Curta complex is CIEA's largest refining operation, located on the Shendan coast near the Tramuntana border, with deep-water port access for crude oil imports and refined product exports.
* '''Distribution''': Fuel distribution networks across Balboa, Potocsí, Tapuya, Lacashe, Asikyira, parts of Sinchew, and Balisca (via COBAL), operating retail petrol stations under the '''PB''' brand, retained from the Petrolí de Balboa era.
* '''Petrochemicals''': Petrochemical production at refineries in Badia Curta and Sanrobonia.
* '''Balisca exports''': Refined petroleum products (gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, lubricants) exported to [[Balisca]] via COBAL, the Baliscan state oil import entity, under a long-term supply contract. COBAL handles 100% of Balisca's oil imports, and CIEA supplies approximately 30% of Balisca's total oil consumption. Petroleum products are not covered by the negative tariff; CIEA's competitiveness in the Balisca market derives from the input-side advantage of cheap crude oil (the overvalued ID makes imports inexpensive) and low energy costs (hydropower from the Yalabirga dams).


